course: dictionary
lesson: 8387
title: Words about health
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Rubella is a noun, the name of an illness. You will see it on its own, with no «a» in front, because it is uncountable: you say rubella, not a rubella. The context sentence, «The MMR vaccine protects against measles, mumps and rubella in two doses in early childhood», gives clues. A vaccine protects you from an illness, and measles, mumps and rubella are three illnesses named together.
explain: Rubella is also called German measles. It is caused by a virus, which is a tiny germ, and it gives a red spotty rash and a fever. The word «against» in the sentence is another clue: the vaccine stops you catching rubella.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The MMR vaccine protects against German measles in two doses in early childhood» still makes sense, so rubella and German measles name the same illness. When you are sure, you can use the word yourself.
fact: Rubella is a noun, and it is uncountable.
fact: Rubella is an illness caused by a virus, also called German measles.
fact: Rubella gives a red spotty rash and a fever.
fact: Rubella is usually mild in children, but it is dangerous for unborn babies.
fact: The MMR vaccine protects against measles, mumps and rubella.
exercise: What is rubella also called? | Mumps | Red fever | *German measles | Chickenpox
exercise: «The MMR vaccine protects against measles, mumps and rubella in two doses in early childhood.» What does the vaccine do? | It gives children a red rash | It makes children taller | It cures a broken arm | *It protects children against rubella
exercise: Rubella is usually mild in children, but why is it dangerous? | *It can harm unborn babies | Children always need an operation | It only affects grown-up hair | It makes teeth fall out
test: Which of these is a symptom of rubella? | A broken arm | *A red spotty rash | Blue hair | A sore tooth from sweets
test: Rubella is uncountable, so which phrase is correct? | a rubella | two rubellas | *some rubella | many rubellas
exam: The sentence «The MMR vaccine protects against measles, mumps and rubella in two doses in early childhood» tells us rubella is | *an illness that a vaccine can protect against | a type of weather | a school subject | a food you can eat for breakfast
